{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|and|uncountable}} The [[colour]] of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between [[green]] and [[violet]] in the visible [[spectrum]] and one of the primary additive colours.
#: {{color panel|0028E9|0096FF}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|page=204|volume=I|passage=Lady Penrhyn was quite handsome enough to have spared one ingredient in her cup of fascination, but, unfortunately, having been married in her teens, she expected to live in them, and, never being reminded by the trials to which her sex is subject, of the flight of years, and the inroads of suffering, expected time to stand still, and the first bloom of existence (the '''blue''' on the plum) to remain as stationary as her own taste, for the pleasures of flirtation.}}
#* {{RQ:Mitchell Cloud Atlas|passage=She watches the yachts in the creamy evening '''blues'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Kristen Rogers|title=Over half of US states are trying to eliminate food dyes. Here’s what you can do now|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/29/health/food-dye-bans-states-wellness|date=2025-03-29|passage=Red No. 3, red No. 40, '''blue''' No. 2 and green No. 3 all have been linked with cancer or tumors in animals. Other sources say red No. 40 and yellow No. 5 and No. 6 contain or may be contaminated with known carcinogens.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261011090009/https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/29/health/food-dye-bans-states-wellness|archivedate=11 October 2026}}
# Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour.
#: {{ux|en|I don't like red Smarties. Have you got a '''blue'''?}}
# A blue dye or pigment.
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Blue clothing.
#: {{ux|en|The boys in '''blue''' marched to the pipers.}}
## {{lb|en|in the plural}} A blue uniform. See {{m|en|blues}}.
## A member of a sports team that wears blue colours; {{lb|en|in the plural}} a nickname for the team as a whole. ''See also ''[[blues]]''.''
##: ''Come on, you '''blues'''!''
## {{lb|en|baseball|slang}} An [[umpire]], in reference to the typical dark-blue colour of the umpire's uniform. ''Sometimes perceived by umpires as [[derogatory]] when used by players or coaches while disputing a call.''
##: ''He was safe! Terrible call, '''blue'''!''
## Sporting colours awarded by a university or other institution for sporting achievement, such as representing one's university, especially and originally at Oxford and Cambridge Universities in England. See also ''[[full blue]]'', ''[[half blue]]''.
##: ''He excelled at rowing and received a '''blue''' in the sport at Oxford.''
## A person who has received such sporting colours.
##: ''He was a rugby '''blue''' at Cambridge.''
## {{lb|en|slang}} A member of [[law enforcement]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2022|author=Jim Malloy|title=Die, Mother Goose, Die
|passage=He dialed Kathy to be sure she was okay and see if the '''blues''' arrived. She was crying when she picked up the phone.<br>“Kathy, honey, I'm here. It'll be okay. Are the police there?”}}
## {{lb|en|now|historical}} A [[bluestocking]].
# The [[sky]], literally or figuratively.
#: {{ux|en|The balloon floated up into the '''blue'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|His request for leave came [[out of the blue|out of the '''blue''']].}}
# The [[ocean]]; deep waters.
# The far [[distance]]; a [[remote]] or [[distant]] place.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=Peter Hathaway Capstick|title=Death in the Long Grass|isbn=1466803924
|passage=The problem with buffalo as well as most African antelopes as a steady diet is that they have very little marbling or body fat and, after six months out in the '''blue''', one dreams at night of a T-bone steak sizzling in great globules of yellow fat.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Thomas C. Barger; Timothy J. Barger|title=Out in the '''Blue''': Letters from Arabia, 1937 to 1940 : a Young American Geologist Explores the Deserts of Early Saudi Arabia|isbn=9780970115737}}
# A dog or cat with a [[slaty]] gray coat.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Joe Stahlkuppe|title=American Pit Bull Terrier Handbook|page=131|passage=On average, '''blues''' and other dilutes have weaker coats and skin problems seem more prevalent in the dilutes.}}
# {{lb|en|snooker}} One of the [[colour]] balls used in [[snooker]], with a value of five points.
# {{senseid|en|butterfly}} {{lb|en|entomology}} Any of the [[butterfly|butterflies]] of the subfamily {{taxfmt|Polyommatinae|subfamily}} in the family {{taxfmt|Lycaenidae|family}}, most of which have blue on their wings.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869|author={{w|Alfred Russel Wallace}}|title={{w|The Malay Archipelago}}|publisher=Macmillan and Co.|year_published=|volume=II|location=London|page=199|passage=The large and handsome spectre-butterfly, Hestia durvillei; the pale-winged peacock butterfly, Drusilla catops; and the most brilliant and wonderful of the clear-winged moths, Cocytia durvillei, were especially interesting, as well, as several little "'''blues'''," equalling in brilliancy and beauty anything the butterfly world can produce.}}
# {{senseid|en|bluefish}} A [[bluefish]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2 Feb 74|volume=1|number=32|journal=Gay Community News|page=20|author=Roger Frye|title=Warm Heart|text=When snow falls on the roses, when a mako is beheaded, when the weaks and '''blues''' swim south}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Lenny Rudow|title=Rudow's Guide to Fishing the Mid Atlantic|page=102|passage='''Blues''' are about as vicious a fish as you'll find on the Atlantic seaboard &mdash; they will continue to slash through schools of bait even after they have eaten so much that they're constantly regurgitating shredded baitfish.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|colloquial}} An argument or brawl.
#* '''2004''', {{w|Tim Winton}}, ''The Turning'' (short stories), Picador UK Paperback edition 2006. Short story,'' 'Small Mercies' '' (at p.91):
#*: "I had a '''blue''' with Dad," said Fay. "He wanted to drive us, I wanted to walk."
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Cheryl Jorgensen|title=The Taint|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=F99evgycTP4C&pg=PA135&dq=%22a+blue+between%22+-intitle:%22blue%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=opL5TvasAqvImQXiv7SCAg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22a%20blue%20between%22%20-intitle%3A%22blue%22&f=false|page=135
|passage=If they had a '''blue''' between themselves, they kept it there, it never flowed out onto the streets to innocent people — like a lot of things that have been happenin′ on the streets today.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=John Gilfoyle|title=Remember Cannon Hill|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=jDeZFM60788C&pg=PA102&dq=%22a+blue+between%22+-intitle:%22blue%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=fY75TpriGKTJmQXb3JHQBQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22a%20blue%20between%22%20-intitle%3A%22blue%22&f=false|page=102
|passage=On another occasion, there was a '''blue''' between Henry Daniels and Merv Wilson down at the pig sale. I don′t know what it was about, it only lasted a minute or so, but they shook hands when it was over and that was the end of it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Julietta Jameson|title=Me, Myself and Lord Byron|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=75tL7CDr-CoC&pg=PT23&dq=%22a+blue+between%22+-intitle:%22blue%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=lZP5TsTfKeqamQWzi9WLAg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22a%20blue%20between%22%20-intitle%3A%22blue%22&f=false|page=unnumbered
|passage=I was a bit disappointed. Was that it? No abuse like Lord Byron had endured? Not that I was wishing that upon myself. It was just that a '''blue''' between my parents, albeit a raging, foul, bile-spitting hate fest, was not exactly Charles Dickens.}}
# {{senseid|en|laundry aid}} A liquid with an intense blue colour, added to a laundry wash to prevent yellowing of white clothes.
#: {{syn|en|blueing#Noun|bluer#English:_blueing for laundry}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1948|author=Alec H. Chisholm|title=Bird Wonders of Australia|page=13|passage=It was applied methodically, carefully, resolutely, as in the fashion of a Satin-bird with charcoal, desiccated wood or '''blue''' from laundry-bags.}}
# Any of several processes to protect metal against rust.
# {{lb|en|British}} A type of [[firecracker]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1781|author=w:Frances Burney|title=Journals & Letters|page=172|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2001
|passage=Lord Lyttelton's Life by Dr Johnson {{...}} which a whole tribe of '''''Blues''''', with Mrs Montagu at their Head, have Vowed to execrate and revenge {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|particle physics}} One of the three [[colour charge]]s for [[quark]]s.
# {{lb|en|UK politics}} A member or supporter of the Conservative Party.
#: {{ux|en|He is a true '''blue'''.}}
# A [[blue cheese]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=J. B. Dann|title=Anecdotal|page=233
|passage="No, just get over here. You need to try this one!" she exclaimed as she cheesed up a chunk of bread with one of our '''blues'''.}}
#* '''2012''', Culture Magazine, Laurel Miller, Thalassa Skinner, ''Cheese For Dummies'' (page 55)
#*: '''Blues''' are made via the introduction of molds from the genus ''Penicillum roqueforti'', which are normally added to the milk toward the beginning of the cheesemaking process.
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} Risqué or pornographic material.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2009-02-25|author=S. Purcell|title=Popular Shakespeare: Simulation and Subversion on the Modern Stage|publisher=Springer|isbn=9780230234222|page=51
|text=Improvising freely, he entered the stage with a karaoke set and introduced himself as a 'Bohemian street performer', before launching into a series of clubstyle gags and one-liners, promising 'a bit of '''blue''' for the Dads'{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2014-02-05|author=Charlie Flowers|title=Kill Order|publisher=Lulu.com|isbn=9781291569865|page=176
|text=Fuzz grinned and nodded at the stage. 'Bit of '''blue''' for the lads.' {{...}} The stage was dimly lit, and populated by Nubian slaves and harem girls in artfully draped deshabille.}}

