===Etymology===
In reference to the physical concept (region of spacetime with extreme gravitational pull), physicist {{w|lang=en|Hong-Yee Chiu}} attributed the term to his colleague {{coin|en|notext=1|nobycat=1|Robert H. Dicke}}, who stated around 1960–1961 that the objects were ''like the {{w|lang=en|Black Hole of Calcutta}}''. The first known usage in print was by journalist Ann Ewing in 1964. Widespread popularization of the term is generally credited to a lecture in 1967 by the physicist {{w|lang=en|John Archibald Wheeler|John Wheeler}}.<ref name="RobertDicke">{{cite-web
|author      = Tom Siegfried
|title       = 50 years later, it’s hard to say who named black holes
|url         = https://www.sciencenews.org/blog/context/50-years-later-it%E2%80%99s-hard-say-who-named-black-holes
