* Probably from the German {{m|de|blitzartig||very fast, like lightning}}

* Another version suggests French blesser (to wound),<ref>{{cite-book|author=Craig M. Carver|year=1991|title=A History of English in Its own words|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YbpZAAAAMAAJ&q=%22I+took+a+blizzard+at+one%22&dq=%22I+took+a+blizzard+at+one%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=v0fMVMndB8jToATd1oD4BQ&ved=0CCoQ6AEwAzgK|page=202|accessdate=Feb 21 2015|isbn=0062700138}}</ref> but neither this nor the German can be substantiated. Yet another claims that blizzard derives from English dialect blizzer, meaning "a blaze" or "flash" ("Put towthry sticks on th' fire, an' let's have a blizzer," - The English Dialect Dictionary) <ref>{{cite-book|author=Joseph Wright|year=1898|title=The English Dialect Dictionary|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=oEMOAQAAMAAJ&pg=PR16&dq=%22English+Dialect+Society%22+Nottinghamshire&hl=en&sa=X&ei=SaTNVICRHYbioASAgIGADA&ved=0CCQQ6AEwAQ#v=onepage&q=blizzer&f=false|page=303|accessdate=Feb 21 2015|isbn=1113929766}}</ref> or from blazer (something that blazes or blasts), which gave the early sense "a volley of firing guns," that is, a general "blazing away."

* [https://books.google.com/books?id=9GECAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA217&dq=%22blizzer%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=9MrJVJHZN8zeoASjmoCYCg&ved=0CEIQ6AEwBw#v=onepage&q=%22blizzer%22&f=false Thomas Ratcliffe of Worksop, Nottinghamshire, in the March 17, 1888, edition]

BLIZZARD (7th S. v. 106).—The word blizzard is well known through the Midlands, and its cognates are fairly numerous. I have known the word and its kin fully thirty years. Country folk use the word to denote blazing, blasting, blinding, dazzling, or stifling. One who has had to face a severe storm of snow, hail, rain, dust, or wind, would say on reaching shelter that he has "faced a blizzer," or that the storm was "a regular blizzard." A blinding flash of lightning would call forth the exclamation, "My! that wor a blizzomer!" or "That wor a blizzer!" "Put towthry sticks on th' fire, an let's have a blizzer"—a blaze. "A good blizzom" = a good blaze. "That tree is blizzared" = blasted, withered. As an oath the word is often used, and "May I be blizzerded" will be readily understood.

* A check of some of the Midlands regional glossaries printed in the 1800s finds several entries for blizzy. First, from Anne Baker, Glossary of Northamptonshire words and phrases (1854): <ref>{{cite-book|author=Anne Baker|title=Glossary of Northamptonshire words and phrases vol. 1|year=1854|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=be8IAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA57&dq=%22Blizzy%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=StrJVMTOGszzoAS6l4Jw&ved=0CCwQ6AEwAzgK#v=onepage&q=%22Blizzy%22&f=false|page=57|accessdate=Feb 21, 2015|isbn=1152470914}}</ref>

BLIZZY. A blaze. "Blow the fire, and let's have a nice blizzy." This, though now considered a vulgarism, is a retention of the original A.-Sax. blysa, a blaze.

And Angelina Parker, A Glossary of Words Used in Oxfordshire (1876): <ref>{{cite-book|author=Angalina Parker|title=A Glossary of Words Used in Oxfordshire|year=1876|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iIAHAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA114&dq=%22Blizzy%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=StrJVMTOGszzoAS6l4Jw&ved=0CCEQ6AEwATgK#v=onepage&q=%22Blizzy%22&f=false|page=114|accessdate=Feb 21 2015|isbn=117864894X}}</ref>

Blizzy, a flaring fire produced by putting on small sticks. Ex. 'Let's 'a a bit of a blizzy afore us goes to bed.'

And from Barzillai Lowsley, A Glossary of Berkshire Words and Phrases (1888): <ref>{{cite-book|author=Barzillai Lowsley|title=A Glossary of Berkshire Words and Phrases|year=1888|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HKITAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA50&dq=%22Blizzy%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=-NnJVOiGK5TVoASR_IHYCA&ved=0CFUQ6AEwCg#v=onepage&q=%22Blizzy%22&f=false|page=80|accessdate=Feb 21 2015|isbn=1248484231}}</ref>

BLIZZY.— A blaze. The fire is said to be all of a "blizzy" when pieces of wood have been inserted amongst the coal to make it burn cheerfully.

And from G. F. Northall, A Warwickshire Word-book (1896): <ref>{{cite-book|author=G. F. Northall|title=A Warwickshire Word-book|year=1896|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Zy4_AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA31&dq=%22Blizzy%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=-NnJVOiGK5TVoASR_IHYCA&ved=0CD8Q6AEwBg#v=onepage&q=%22Blizzy%22&f=false|page=31|accessdate=Feb 21 2015}}</ref>

Blizzy, sb. A blaze, a blast, a flare of fire. A.-Sax. blysa, a blaze. Common.

They suggest that blizzy survived from the ancient word blysa in numerous localities and might well share a root with the U.S. blizzard.
