===Etymology 3===
[[File:Chance_and_a_Half,_Posing.jpg|thumb|horse with a blaze (1)]]
[[File:Balise de sentier de Grande Randonnée.jpg|thumb|a blaze from the {{w|GR footpath}}]]

A 1639 borrowing, perhaps from {{bor|en|nl|bles}} or {{bor|en|gml|blesse}}, {{m|gml|bles}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*blasī}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*blasį̄}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*blasaz|t=white, pale (of animals)}} + {{m|gem-pro|*-į̄|pos=forming nouns}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰel-|t=shiny, white}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=7772101247}}</ref><ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|pos=n.2|v=13585}}</ref> Cognate with {{cog|de|Blesse}}, {{cog|sv|bläs}}. The verb is from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''v.''{{sup|3}}|noformat=1|id=1045688623}}</ref><ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|pos=v.3|v=43292}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The white or lighter-coloured [[marking]]s on a horse's face.
#: {{ux|en|The palomino had a white '''blaze''' on its face.}}
# A spot made on trees by chipping off a piece of the bark, usually as a surveyor's mark.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The New Purchase: Or, Early Years in the Far West - Page 71| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=rEVBAQAAMAAJ| author=w:Baynard Rush Hall| year=1855| passage=The '''blaze''' is a longitudinal cut on trees at convenient intervals, made by cutting off the bark with an axe or hatchet: three '''blazes''' in a perpendicular line on the same tree indicating a legislative road, the single '''blaze''', a settlement or neighbourhood road.}}
# {{senseid|en|hiking}} {{lb|en|hiking}} A [[waymark]]: any [[marking]] as painted on trees, carvings, affixed markers, posts, flagging, or crosses placed to lead hikers on their trail.

