===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰleh₃-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|blome}}, from {{der|en|non|blóm}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*blōmô||flower}}. {{doublet|en}} of ''[[#Etymology_3|bloom]]'' (“spongy mass of metal”); see there for more.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[blossom]]; the [[flower]] of a [[plant]]; an expanded [[bud]].
#* {{RQ:Prescott Mexico
|passage=the rich '''blooms''' and enamelled vegetation of the tropics}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2021<span style="font-weight: normal;"> (originally published 2017, adapted 2021)</span>|author=[[wikipedia:David Elliot Grann|David Grann]]|title=[[wikipedia:Killers of the Flower Moon (book)|Killers of the Flower Moon: Adapted for Younger Readers]]|page=1|passage=In May, when coyotes howl beneath an unnervingly large moon, taller plant, such as spiderworts and black-eyed Susans, begin to creep over the tinier '''blooms''', stealing their light and water.}}
# {{lb|en|collective}} [[flower|Flowers]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The opening of flowers in general; the state of blossoming or of having the flowers open.
#: {{ux|en|The cherry trees are in '''bloom'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=3|passage=sight of vernal '''bloom'''|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|II||O, not for thee the glow, the '''bloom''',<br>⁠Who changest not in any gale,<br>⁠Nor branding summer suns avail<br>To touch thy thousand years of gloom: […]}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A state or time of beauty, freshness, and [[vigor]]; an opening to higher [[perfection]], analogous to that of buds into blossoms.
#: {{syn|en|prime}}
#: {{co|en|the '''bloom''' of youth}}
#* {{RQ:Hawthorne Scarlet Letter|passage=every successive mother had transmitted to her child a fainter '''bloom''', a more delicate and briefer beauty.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|lyricist=Kurt Cobain|title=In Bloom|album=Nevermind|artist=Nirvana|year=1992|passage=We can have some more / Nature is a whore / Bruises on the fruit / Tender age in '''bloom'''}}
# Rosy colour; the flush or glow on a person's cheek.
# The delicate, powdery [[coating]] upon certain growing or newly-gathered [[fruit]]s or leaves, as on grapes, plums, etc.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|author=Donna Pliner Rodnitzky|title=Low-Carb Smoothies
|passage=The '''bloom''' on blueberries is the dusty powder that protects them from the Sun; it does not rinse off.}}
# Anything giving an appearance of attractive [[freshness]].
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|chapter=2|passage=a new, fresh, brilliant world, with all the '''bloom''' upon it}}
# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} An [[algal bloom]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=w:Tim Flannery|title=Europe: A Natural History|page=28|passage=Where upwellings or other sources of nutrients allow Ehux to abound, it can proliferate, as '''blooms''', to the point that the ocean turns milky.}}
# {{lb|en|zoology|collective}} A group of [[jellyfish]], especially one caused by a swift increase in the population and especially when they are stranded on shore; the occurrence of such a bloom, and the ecological impact it has.
# The clouded appearance which [[varnish]] sometimes takes upon the surface of a picture.
# A yellowish deposit or powdery coating which appears on well-tanned leather.<ref>{{R:Knight AMD}}</ref>
# {{lb|en|mineralogy}} A bright-hued variety of some minerals.
#: {{co|en|the rose-red cobalt '''bloom'''}}
# {{lb|en|culinary}} A white area of [[cocoa butter]] that forms on the surface of [[chocolate]] when warmed and cooled.
# A [[natural]] [[protective]] [[coating]] on an [[eggshell]].
# {{lb|en|television}} An undesirable [[halo]] [[effect]] that may occur when a very [[bright]] region is displayed next to a very [[dark]] region of the screen.
# {{lb|en|video games}} The increase in [[bullet]] spread over time as a gun's [[trigger]] is kept held.
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://critpoints.net/2018/02/21/bullet-bloom-an-fps-tragedy/|date=February 21, 2018|title=Bullet Bloom: An FPS Tragedy|work=CritPoints|text='''Bloom''' does add a skill element, burst firing the weapon instead of holding the trigger down, but it's questionable if adding a skill element like that is the actual intent of '''bloom''', because that’s a pretty lame and linear skill element to add, requiring people to tap the button instead of hold it down.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260309125809/https://critpoints.net/2018/02/21/bullet-bloom-an-fps-tragedy/|archivedate=9 March 2026}}
# {{lb|en|Philippines|fandom slang|usually|in the plural}} A [[fan]] of Filipino [[girl group]] {{w|Bini (group)|BINI}}.
# {{lb|en|collective}} A large [[swarm]] of [[ladybug]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=A gaggle, a confusion and a conspiracy - bizarre animal collective group names|magazine=w:BBC|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/newsround/48539466|date=2019-06-06|passage=Thousands of the little red and black insects took flight at the same time, and the '''bloom''' stretched for 10 miles before they landed.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260827095118/https://www.bbc.co.uk/newsround/48539466|archivedate=27 August 2026}}
#* {{quote-web|en|passage=Though the '''bloom''' appeared to be a massive 80 miles by 80 miles on the radar, Dandrea believes the lady beetles were not flying in a concentrated mass that big.|date=June 7, 2019|url=https://www.dogonews.com/2019/6/7/rain-cloud-on-weather-radar-turns-out-to-be-a-ladybug-swarm|author=Meera D.|site=DoGo News|title="Rain Cloud" On Weather Radar Turns Out To Be A Ladybug Swarm|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260801074529/https://www.dogonews.com/2019/6/7/rain-cloud-on-weather-radar-turns-out-to-be-a-ladybug-swarm|archivedate=1 August 2026}}
# A residue left on a surface as a result of a chemical process.
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=A thin yellowish "'''bloom'''" covered the film, but this polished off as before, and the metallic silver took a perfectly "black" polish.|page=162|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Memoirs_of_the_Royal_Astronomical_Societ/UdzNAAAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22was+scrubbed%22&pg=PA162&printsec=frontcover|publisher=Blackwell Scientific|author=Royal Astronomical Society|volume=50-51|title=Memoirs of the Royal Astronomical Society|year=1892}}

